rcvd is a privacy-first encrypted DNS engine: a single Go binary driven by
purpose-built configuration files. It speaks DNS over QUIC (DoQ), DNS over
TLS (DoT), and DNS over HTTPS (DoH/DoH3), and never falls back to cleartext
DNS. If every encrypted upstream fails it returns SERVFAIL rather than leaking
a query over port 53.

Features include multi-upstream fallback with health checks, DNSSEC
validation, domain blocklists and allowlists (plain-list and hosts formats,
with wildcard support; an allowlist switches to default-deny), a built-in
cache, runtime statistics, and both forwarder and encrypted-upstream service
modes. TLS can be automated via ACME (certmagic) so any encrypted endpoint
(DoH, DoT, or DoQ) presents a real CA certificate with a DNS-ID in its SAN.

A forwarder normally verifies its upstream against the Web-PKI, so a Mode 1
instance can point at any public CA-issued resolver unchanged. For a private
link between two rcvd instances on a trusted LAN, it can instead pin the
upstream service's public key (SPKI pin), accepting a self-signed certificate
while still failing closed on any key mismatch.
